  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1$\nintersects the line $y=x.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{20} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
